---
import Row from '../components/Row.astro'
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
---

<Base
  title="Manifesto"
  description="What Tim de Vries believes about AI, craft and the things worth building."
>
  <Row label="Manifesto">
    <article class="lead">
      <h1>For a more human future.</h1>

      <p>
        I believe the most important thing we can build is a world worth living in. One with
        more room for curiosity, care, and the kind of work that makes us feel human.
      </p>
      <p>
        Technology is part of how we get there. I embrace AI with conviction. It helps me move
        faster, explore further, and put ideas into practice that once sat beyond reach. I want
        to use that possibility well.
      </p>

      <h2>The human hand</h2>
      <p>
        As more of the world can be generated, the things made with real attention will matter
        more. The judgment of a designer. The patience of an engineer. The instinct of someone
        who has spent years understanding their craft. The care you can feel in something
        before you can explain it.
      </p>
      <p>
        That kind of craftsmanship is becoming more precious than ever. Tools can multiply what
        we produce. People give that work meaning. My job is to bring the two together with
        taste, honesty and intention.
      </p>

      <h2>The care in making</h2>
      <p>
        There are ideas in Japanese craft I keep returning to. Monozukuri, the practice and
        spirit of making things, asks you to care about the whole process: how something works,
        how it feels, how it lasts, and what it asks of the people who use it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Shokunin, the craftsperson, is a reminder of the commitment behind good work: learning,
        practising, and taking responsibility for what leaves your hands. Wabi-sabi adds another
        lesson: beauty can live in imperfection, in change, and in the traces of a life actually
        lived.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I welcome powerful tools and keep asking human questions. Is this useful? Is it
        thoughtful? Does it belong here? Has it made something better?
      </p>

      <h2>What I choose to build</h2>
      <p>
        Systems that give people time back. I automate repeated work, connect what is
        fragmented, and bring clarity to what feels complicated. I use AI wherever it helps, and
        keep people involved wherever judgment, relationships and responsibility belong.
      </p>
      <p>
        The measure is the effect on the world around it. A team with more energy. A business
        that serves people better. A product that feels considered. Hours returned to someone
        who has better things to do.
      </p>
      <p>That is the future I want to help make. More capable, more thoughtful, and more human.</p>
    </article>
  </Row>
</Base>
